[StudyName "Reti - Reversed Benko"]

1. Nf3 d5 2. c4 d4 3. g3 c5 4. b4 b6 (4... Nc6 5. b5 Nb8 (5... Na5 6. d3 { [%cal Gd3c4] } 6... b6 7. Bg2 { Starting with Bd2 might be stronger, but Bg2 probably less possible continuations. } { [%cal Gc1d2,Bf1g2] } 7... Bb7 8. Bd2 Nf6 9. Bxa5 bxa5 10. Qa4 { Black has no easy way to defend a5 from our queen and knight. } { [%csl Gb3][%cal Gb1d2,Gd2b3] } 10... Qb6 11. Nbd2 e6 12. Nb3 { Next we'll grab the pawn with the knight, unless black plays a6 - then we grab with the queen: () } { [%csl Ga5][%cal Gb3a5,Ba7a6,Ba4a5] } 12... a6 13. Qxa5 Qd6 14. a4 axb5 15. Qxb5+ Bc6 16. Nfxd4!! { Only move! We need to sack the queen. All other moves are losing.
However, it's fine because... } 16... Bxb5 17. Nxb5 Qe5 { Trying to protect the rook with the queen doesn't work due to the fork: (17... Qd8 18. Bxa8 Qxa8 19. Nc7+) } { [%csl Ra8,Gc7][%cal Rd6d8,Gb5c7,Rd8a8] } 18. Bxa8 Qb2 19. O-O Qxb3 20. a5 { Black can't stop our pawn. Also important that they'll have backrank problems if they castle and take the new queen.

For example: (20... Be7 21. Bc6+ Nd7 22. a6 O-O 23. Bxd7 Bf6 24. Ra3 Qb2 25. a7 Qxe2 26. a8=Q) and black can't take the queen due to mate. } { [%cal Bf8e7,Ba8c6,Ga5a6,Ga6a7,Ga7a8] }) 6. Bg2 Nf6 7. O-O Nbd7 { The most common continuation,  we respond with the same plan against other moves like g6 & e6 } 8. e3 e5 9. exd4 exd4 (9... cxd4 10. Re1 Bd6 11. Nxd4 O-O 12. Nb3 Nc5 13. d4 { [%cal Gc5b3,Ga2b3,Be5d4,Bd1d4] }) 10. Re1+ Be7 11. Qe2 Nf8 (11... Kf8 12. Ba3 Bd6 13. Nxd4) 12. d3 Ne6 13. Ng5 O-O 14. Nxe6 fxe6 15. Bg5 { We don't mind trading the bishop for the knight. 
Next we can continue with Nd2 and a4.
White's position is much better (1.7+) } { [%cal Gb1d2,Ga2a4,Bh7h6,Bg5f6] }) (4... e6 5. Bb2 { Now black can't capture on b4 as the d-pawn would be hanging.
Which baits them to play Nc6... } 5... Nc6 (5... Nf6 6. Bg2 Be7 { If Nc6 - we chase it with b4 as usual and then play e3 after castling. } { [%cal Gb8c6,Gb4b5,Be2e3] } 7. O-O O-O 8. d3 Nc6 9. b5 Nb8 10. a4 Nbd7 11. e3 e5 12. a5) 6. b5 Nce7 7. e3! { Important move, without it we lose a big part of our advantage. } 7... dxe3 (7... Nf5 8. Bg2 Nf6 9. O-O Be7 10. exd4 cxd4 11. d3 O-O 12. a4 Rb8 13. Nbd2 b6) 8. dxe3 { Trading queens is not a problem for us - black's pieces are terrible and we're dominating the queenside. } { [%csl Ra8,Rc8,Rf8,Rg8,Rh8,Gb5,Ga4,Gc4][%cal Ga2a4] } 8... Qxd1+ 9. Kxd1 b6 10. Bg2 Bb7 11. a4 O-O-O+ 12. Ke2 Nf5 13. a5 Nf6 14. axb6 axb6 15. Nbd2 Be7 { One of many moves, our trap still applies to most moves:(15... Bd6 16. Ra7 Bb8 17. Rxb7 Kxb7 18. Ng5+) } (15... Ne4 16. Ra7 Nxd2 17. Rxb7 Kxb7 18. Nxd2+ Kc7 19. Ra1 { Next we're coming in with the rook and bishop! } { [%csl Ga7][%cal Ga1a7,Gg2c6] }) 16. Ra7 Kb8 17. Rxb7+ Kxb7 18. Ng5+ { Ne5+ -> Ra1 is slightly stronger... but no need to complicate things.This version is more common in other variations without Be7. } { [%csl Gf7][%cal Gg5f7] }) (4... Nf6 5. Bg2 { We could try to punish 4... Nf6 - but it's more practical to try and transpose. We should be happy to get a benko mainline, especially with white. } 5... e6 6. Bb2 { Same idea as in the earlier version - black can't take on b4 as d4 would hang (baiting Nc6) } 6... Bd6 { Be7 is more common, but has been covered in another move order. } 7. O-O O-O 8. d3 Nc6 9. b5 Ne7 10. a4 Rb8 11. Nbd2 b6 12. e3 e5 13. Ng5 Bb7 14. Bxb7 Rxb7 15. Nge4 { Next we'll likely trade once on e4, then we can play exd3. } { [%cal Gf6e4,Gd2e4,Be3d4] }) 5. bxc5 bxc5 6. Nxd4!! { Another fancy move that's not available in the original benko } 6... cxd4 (6... Qxd4 7. Bg2 Qxa1 8. Nc3 Bd7 9. Bxa8 Bc6 10. Bxc6+ Nxc6 11. O-O e6 12. Qa4 Kd7 13. Ba3) (6... Bb7 7. Nf3 Bxf3 8. exf3 Nc6 9. Bg2 { [%cal Gf3f4,Gb1c3,Ge1g1] } 9... Qd4 10. f4 Rc8 11. Qa4 { Taking our rook is bad for black: (11... Qxa1 12. Bxc6+ Kd8 13. Nc3 Qxc1+ 14. Ke2 Qb2 15. Rb1) }) 7. Bg2 Bd7 8. Bxa8 Nc6 { Most common is Bc6 - but that makes it even more simple for us. } 9. Bxc6 Bxc6 10. O-O { Yes, it's still legal } 10... e5 11. e4!! Bxe4 { If black doesn't take, we simply play d3 and continue development. } 12. Qe2 Nf6 13. d3 { [%cal Ge2e5] } *
